\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\b\f0\fs28\lang9 UPDATES TO THE FOOD PANTRY DATABASE\b0\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\b TRIGGERS\par
\b0 05/6/2025 - Amended the trigger to update stock levels after issuing a food parcel.\par
The original trigger was checking NEW.food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id twice\f1\emdash once for updating total_food_parcels_issued and again for total_food_parcels_remaining. \par
IF NEW.food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id THEN\par
    UPDATE food_parcels_for_issue\par
    SET total_food_parcels_issued = (total_food_parcels_issued + NEW.amount_issued)\par
    WHERE food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id; \par
END IF;\par
\par
IF NEW.food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id THEN\par
    UPDATE food_parcels_for_issue\par
    SET total_food_parcels_remaining = (total_food_parcels_remaining - NEW.amount_issued)\par
    WHERE food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id; \par
END IF;\par
\f0\lang2057\par
\b\lang9 05/6/2025 \b0\lang2057 M\f1\lang9 erge\f0\lang2057 d\f1\lang9  the\f0\lang2057  above \f1\lang9 updates into a single UPDATE statement to \f0\lang2057 remove redundant code and \f1\lang9 streamline the logic.\par
\f0 IF NEW.food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id THEN\par
    UPDATE food_parcels_for_issue\par
    SET total_food_parcels_issued = total_food_parcels_issued + NEW.amount_issued,\par
        total_food_parcels_remaining = total_food_parcels_remaining - NEW.amount_issued\par
    WHERE food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id; \par
END IF;\par
\par
This way, the database only performs one UPDATE operation instead of two, improving efficiency while maintaining the same logic.\par
\par
\b CONCURRENCY CONTROLS \par
\b0 Concurrency issues arise when multiple transactions try to access or modify the same data at the same time, leading to potential inconsistencies\f1\emdash this is called a race condition.\par
For example, imagine two people issuing a food parcel at the same time. If your database doesn't handle concurrency well, both transactions might read the same stock level, update it independently, and overwrite each other, causing incorrect totals.\par
To prevent this, you can use locking or transaction control techniques, such as:\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li360\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Row-level locking (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE): Ensures only one transaction can modify a row at a time.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Optimistic locking: Uses version numbers or timestamps to detect conflicts before committing changes.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b\f0 05/6/2025 \b0 - Added \f1 FOR UPDATE\f0\lang2057 ; for \lang9 concurrency controls. This locks the row before updating preventing race conditions when multiple transactions try to update the same food parcel. This ensures stock levels remain accurate even when multiple transactions happen simultaneously. \par
\b 05/6/2025 \b0 - Added Timeout (SET innodb_lock_wait_timeout = 5;)\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Prevents long waits when a row is locked by another transaction, reducing the risk of deadlocks.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}This ensures that if a transaction can't acquire a lock within 5 seconds, it fails instead of waiting indefinitely.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b 05/6/2025 \b0 - Implemented Error Handling (DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION / SQLSTATE '40001')\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Ensures failures like deadlocks or unexpected errors don\f1\rquote\f0 t corrupt data.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}If a deadlock (SQLSTATE '40001') or another error occurs, the system triggers an error message instead of allowing faulty updates.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b 05/6/2025 \b0 - Replaced Hardcoded IDs such as FP01 with NEW.food_parcel_id\par
Allows the trigger to dynamically handle any food parcel, instead of being limited to one (FP01).\par
NEW.food_parcel_id pulls the ID directly from the newly inserted row, making the trigger work for all food parcels without extra triggers.\par
\par
Here\f1\rquote\f0 s a good approach:\par
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS TR_update_food_parcels_stock_AFTER_issue_NORMAL_diet_food_parcel;\par
DELIMITER $$\par
CREATE TRIGGER TR_update_food_parcels_stock_AFTER_issue_NORMAL_diet_food_parcel\par
AFTER INSERT ON members_issued_food_parcels\par
FOR EACH ROW\par
BEGIN\par
    -- Declare variables FIRST\par
    DECLARE v_food_parcel_id VARCHAR(20);\par
   \par
    -- Declare error handlers for rollback\par
    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '40001'\par
    BEGIN\par
        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' \par
        SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Deadlock detected: Transaction rolled back. Try again later.';\par
    END;\par
\par
    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\par
    BEGIN\par
        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' \par
        SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'An unexpected error occurred. Transaction rolled back.';\par
    END; \par
    \par
    -- Set timeout to avoid long waits for locks\par
    SET innodb_lock_wait_timeout = 5; \par
\par
    -- Lock the correct row before updating  \par
    SELECT food_parcel_id INTO v_food_parcel_id \par
    FROM food_parcels_for_issue \par
    WHERE food_parcel_id = NEW.food_parcel_id\par
    FOR UPDATE;\par
\par
    -- Ensure we're updating the correct parcel\par
    IF NEW.food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id THEN\par
        UPDATE food_parcels_for_issue\par
        SET total_food_parcels_issued = total_food_parcels_issued + NEW.amount_issued,\par
            total_food_parcels_remaining = total_food_parcels_remaining - NEW.amount_issued\par
        WHERE food_parcel_id = v_food_parcel_id; \par
    END IF;\par
    \par
END $$\par
DELIMITER ;\par
\par
\b Why It Works:\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li360\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b0 FOR UPDATE locks the row, preventing other transactions from changing it until the trigger completes.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Reduces race conditions, ensuring your stock levels remain accurate.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Error handling (DECLARE EXIT HANDLER) ensures deadlocks and unexpected errors don\f1\rquote\f0 t disrupt updates.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b Key Additions:\par
\b0\f2\lang2057\u9989?\f1  \f0 FOR UPDATE; \f3\u8594?\f1  \f0 Locks the row, preventing other transactions from modifying \lang9 until this one is done.\par
\f2\u9989?\f1  \f0 DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION \f3\u8594? Handles errors without rollback, ensuring failed transactions don't corrupt data.\f0\lang9\par
\par
\b Why Use This?\par
Ensures data consistency \b0\f3\u8594? Prevents incomplete updates from causing discrepancies in food parcel stock levels.\par
\b Reduces race conditions \b0\u8594? Other transactions must wait until this one completes before making changes.\par
\b Improves stability \b0\u8594? Transactions no longer hang indefinitely due to deadlocks, increasing system reliability.\par
This refined version keeps your database efficient, avoids concurrency issues, and ensures smooth execution!\par
\f0\lang9\par
\b HOW TO TELL IF A DATABASE SUPPORTS TRANSACTIONS?\par
\b0 MySQL:\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 InnoDB supports transactions.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}MyISAM does not support transactions.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
Run this command to check\par
SHOW TABLE STATUS WHERE Name = 'your_table_name';\par
If Engine is InnoDB, transactions are supported.\par
\par
\b ROLL BACKS AND COMMIT\b0\par
MySQL triggers do not support rollback, but stored procedures do. If a failure occurs, the stored procedure can undo all changes, preventing data corruption.\par
\par
\b 05/6/2025 - \b0 improved the trigger functionality by wrapping its logic inside a stored procedure, allowing errors handling THEN using a trigger to call the stored procedure. Instead of handling stock updates directly in the trigger, the trigger calls the stored procedure, making the system more reliable and scalable.\par
\par
\b Step-by-Step Execution\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li360\sa200\sl276\slmult1 The trigger fires automatically when a \b new laptop or food parcel entry \b0 is added to incoming_laptop_computers, incoming_food_parcels or any other appropriate tables that store food parcel and laptop quantities.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}The trigger calls the stored procedure, passing \b the laptop ID or food parcel ID \b0 along with the received quantity, ensuring stock levels are updated correctly for both items.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}The stored procedure begins execution and starts a transaction, ensuring all updates happen as a single unit.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}A timeout is set (SET innodb_lock_wait_timeout = 5;) to prevent delays if another transaction is locking the row.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Row locking (FOR UPDATE) ensures that only one transaction can modify stock levels at a time.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}The stock levels update, increasing total_laptops_remaining by the newly received laptop quantity.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}If an error occurs, the procedure triggers rollback (ROLLBACK;), ensuring faulty updates do not persist, and raises a custom error message (SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000').\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}If no errors occur, the transaction is committed (COMMIT;), finalizing all stock updates safely.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b Why This Approach Works Well\b0\par

\pard{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf10\pnindent0{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f4 More reliable stock updates \f3\u8594? Ensures transactions fully complete, avoiding partial or incomplete updates that could cause discrepancies.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Efficient row locking (FOR UPDATE;) \u8594? Prevents race conditions when multiple transactions try updating stock simultaneously, ensuring smooth execution.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Lightweight trigger \u8594? The trigger only calls the procedure, instead of managing transactions directly, making maintenance easier and reducing complexity.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Ensures data integrity \u8594? Prevents incomplete updates from affecting stock levels by committing changes only when all conditions are met.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Improves error handling \u8594? Allows MySQL to properly manage failures, ensuring database consistency without corruption.\par
{\pntext\f10\'B7\tab}Optimizes execution \u8594? The combination of rollback, timeout settings, and transaction control ensures safe, efficient, and scalable concurrency handling.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 This approach strengthens the reliability of stock updates, prevents inconsistencies, and keeps operations running smoothly.\par
\par
\b\f5\lang2057 5/6/2025\b0  - \b updated Sproc_issue_a_FOOD_parcel_IF_one_is_due by adding local variables:\par
\b0\f4     -- Declare local variables\par
    DECLARE v_amount_issued INT;\par
    DECLARE v_date_last_issued DATE;\par
\par
  -- Assign default values if NULL\par
    SET v_amount_issued = IFNULL(p_amount_issued, 1);\par
    SET v_date_last_issued = IFNULL(p_date_last_issued, CURDATE());\par
\par
Declaring local variables like v_amount_issued and v_date_last_issued is important because MySQL does not allow modifications to IN parameters directly.\par
\par
Without local variables, when you try to do:\par
SET amount_issued = IFNULL(amount_issued, 1);\par
MySQL may not correctly assign the new value because IN parameters are read-only inside a stored procedure.\par
\par
Why Local Variables Help\par
Ensures proper assignment \f3\u8594? Local variables allow you to store modified values without restrictions.\par
Prevents unexpected behavior \u8594? If MySQL treats IN parameters as read-only, calculations or updates could fail.\par
Improves clarity \u8594? Using local variables makes debugging and future modifications easier.\par
By declaring v_amount_issued and v_date_last_issued, you ensure the procedure correctly assigns default values before inserting data.\par
\par
\b\f5\lang2057 5/6/2025\b0  - \b updated Sproc_issue_a_FOOD_parcel_IF_one_is_due as shown below \b0 :The updated stored procedure now includes several improvements to enhance data integrity, and stock validation before issuing a food parcel.\b\par
ADDED\par
Error Handling (DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION)\par
\b0 Captures unexpected errors during execution.\par
Triggers a rollback if an error occurs, preventing incomplete transactions.\par
Raises a custom error message (SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000').\par
\par
\b Stock Check Before Issuing Parcels\par
\b0 Queries available stock (SELECT total_food_parcels_remaining) before proceeding.\par
If stock is insufficient, it triggers an error and rolls back the transaction.\par
Prevents issuing more food parcels than available inventory.\par
\par
\b Row Locking (FOR UPDATE)\b0\par
Ensures stock levels cannot be modified by multiple transactions at the same time.\par
Prevents race conditions when several users try issuing food parcels simultaneously.\par
\par
\b Deducting Issued Amount After Confirmation\par
\b0 After verifying stock, the procedure reduces the total available food parcels (UPDATE food_parcels_for_issue).\par
Ensures accurate stock tracking only after a successful issuance.\f4\par
\f0\lang9\par
\par
\b 06/06/2025\b0  - updated the stored procedure as follows:-\par
DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS SP_update_food_parcels_stock_AFTER_ISSUING_food_parcels;\par
\par
DELIMITER $$\par
\par
CREATE PROCEDURE SP_update_food_parcels_stock_AFTER_ISSUING_food_parcels(\par
    IN p_food_parcel_id VARCHAR(20), \par
    IN p_amount_issued INT\par
)\par
\par
BEGIN\par
    -- Declare variables\par
    DECLARE dummy_var VARCHAR(20);\par
    \par
    -- Declare error handlers (without rollback)\par
    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '40001'\par
    BEGIN\par
        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' \par
        SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Deadlock detected: Operation stopped.';\par
    END;\par
\par
    DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\par
    BEGIN\par
        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000' \par
        SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Unexpected error occurred.';\par
    END;\par
    \par
    -- Lock row before updating\par
    SELECT food_parcel_id INTO dummy_var\par
    FROM food_parcels_for_issue\par
    WHERE food_parcel_id = p_food_parcel_id\par
    FOR UPDATE;\par
       \par
    -- Perform the update\par
    UPDATE food_parcels_for_issue\par
    SET total_food_parcels_issued = total_food_parcels_issued + p_amount_issued,\par
        total_food_parcels_remaining = total_food_parcels_remaining - p_amount_issued\par
    WHERE food_parcel_id = p_food_parcel_id;\par
    \par
END $$\par
\par
DELIMITER ;\par
\par
This stored procedure updates stock levels for food parcels after issuance, ensuring data integrity while handling potential errors.\par
\f6 1\f7\u-497?\u8419? \f8\lang2057   \f9\lang9 Check if the Procedure Exists & Drop it (if necessary)\par
DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS ensures that if the procedure already exists, it gets removed before redefining it.\par
\par
\f6 2\f7\u-497?\u8419? \f8\lang2057  \f9\lang9 Declare Variables\par
dummy_var VARCHAR(20); is used to store the food_parcel_id temporarily.\par
\par
\f6 3\f7\u-497?\u8419? \f8\lang2057  \f9\lang9 Handle Errors with SIGNAL SQLSTATE Instead of Rollback\par
Deadlock Detection: If a deadlock occurs (SQLSTATE '40001'), the procedure stops execution and raises an error.\par
General SQL Errors: If any other database error occurs, an error message is signaled.\par
\par
\f6 4\f7\u-497?\u8419? \f8\lang2057  \f9\lang9 Lock the Row Before Updating (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE)\par
Prevents concurrent modifications to the same row, ensuring only one transaction updates stock at a time.\par
The value is stored in dummy_var, but it isn\rquote t actually used\emdash just locked.\par
\par
\f6 5\f7\u-497?\u8419?\f8\lang2057   \f9\lang9 Update Stock Levels in food_parcels_for_issue\par
total_food_parcels_issued increases by the amount issued.\par
total_food_parcels_remaining decreases by the amount issued.\par
This ensures accurate tracking of available stock.\par
\par
\b\f8\lang2057 WHEN DOES THE ABOVE FP RUN\b0\f9\lang9\par
Your stored procedure (SP_update_food_parcels_stock_AFTER_ISSUING_food_parcels) runs whenever it is called explicitly\emdash either from an application or automatically via the trigger.\par
When Does It Execute?\par
\f6 1\f7\u-497?\u8419? \f8\lang2057   \f9\lang9 When Your Trigger Fires Automatically\par
The trigger TR_update_food_parcels_stock_AFTER_ISSUING_food_parcels is set to run AFTER INSERT on members_issued_food_parcels.\par
Whenever a new row is inserted into members_issued_food_parcels, MySQL calls the stored procedure, updating stock levels accordingly.\par
\f0\par
\par
\b 02/08/2026 - ADDED A NEW STORED PROCEDURE - SP_create_address - FOR CREATING ADDRESSES\b0\par
\b\f9 What it is:\par
\b0 A production\_grade MySQL stored procedure that safely creates a new address record, generates a unique address_id using the prefix A, handles concurrency with row\_level locking, retries deadlocked transactions, applies a short lock\_wait timeout, distinguishes duplicate\_address violations using a dedicated SQLSTATE 23000 handler, rolls back cleanly on all other SQL errors, and returns both the full address data and an explicit o_sqlstate OUT parameter so the application can reliably determine whether the address was created (00000), already existed (23000), failed due to another SQL exception (HY000), or ultimately failed after exhausting all retry attempts (45000).\par
\b Race\_Safe Stored Procedure: sp_create_address\par
\b0 DROP PROCEDURE IF EXISTS SP_create_address\par
\par
DELIMITER $$\par
\par
CREATE PROCEDURE SP_create_address(\par
    IN p_house_number_or_name VARCHAR(15),\par
    IN p_street_name  VARCHAR(50),\par
    IN p_city_or_town VARCHAR(50),\par
    IN p_post_code    VARCHAR(10),\par
    IN p_country      VARCHAR(10),\par
    OUT o_address_id  VARCHAR(10),\par
    OUT o_house_number_or_name VARCHAR(15),\par
    OUT o_street_name  VARCHAR(50),\par
    OUT o_city_or_town VARCHAR(50),\par
    OUT o_post_code    VARCHAR(10),\par
    OUT o_country      VARCHAR(10),\par
    OUT o_sqlstate VARCHAR(5)\par
)\par
BEGIN\par
    DECLARE v_next_id VARCHAR(10);\par
    DECLARE v_max_num INT DEFAULT NULL;\par
    DECLARE v_attempts INT DEFAULT 0;\par
    DECLARE v_done INT DEFAULT 0;\par
\par
    -- NEW: Set lock wait timeout to 5 seconds\par
    SET innodb_lock_wait_timeout = 5;\par
\par
    -- NEW: Retry loop (max 3 attempts)\par
    WHILE v_done = 0 AND v_attempts < 3 DO\par
        SET v_attempts = v_attempts + 1;\par
\par
        BEGIN        \par
\tab\tab\tab -- NEW: Duplicate key handler (SQLSTATE 23000)\par
\tab\tab\tab DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '23000'\par
\tab\tab\tab BEGIN\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab ROLLBACK;\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab -- Return attempted address so Java can detect ADDRESS EXISTS\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET o_address_id = NULL;\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET o_house_number_or_name = p_house_number_or_name;\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET o_street_name = p_street_name;\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET o_city_or_town = p_city_or_town;\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET o_post_code = p_post_code;\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET o_country = p_country;\par
                SET o_sqlstate = '23000'; -- duplicate address\par
\par
\tab\tab\tab\tab SET v_done = 1;  -- Stop retrying\par
\tab\tab\tab END;      \par
        \par
        \par
            -- Deadlock handler (SQLSTATE 40001)\par
            DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLSTATE '40001'\par
            BEGIN\par
                ROLLBACK;\par
                -- Retry by continuing the loop\par
            END;\par
\par
            -- Generic error handler\par
            DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\par
            BEGIN\par
                ROLLBACK;\par
                SET o_address_id = NULL;\par
                SET o_house_number_or_name = NULL;\par
                SET o_street_name = NULL;\par
                SET o_city_or_town = NULL;\par
                SET o_post_code = NULL;\par
                SET o_country = NULL;\par
                SET o_sqlstate = 'HY000'; -- other SQL error\par
                SET v_done = 1; -- Stop retrying\par
            END;\par
\par
            START TRANSACTION;\par
\par
            -- Row-level lock on the highest ID row (safe under concurrency)\par
            SELECT CAST(SUBSTRING(address_id, 2) AS UNSIGNED)\par
            INTO v_max_num\par
            FROM members_address\par
            ORDER BY CAST(SUBSTRING(address_id, 2) AS UNSIGNED) DESC\par
            LIMIT 1\par
            FOR UPDATE;\par
\par
            -- Generate next ID using prefix A\par
            IF v_max_num IS NULL THEN\par
                SET v_next_id = 'A0';\par
            ELSE\par
                SET v_next_id = CONCAT('A', v_max_num + 1);\par
            END IF;\par
\par
            -- Insert new address\par
            INSERT INTO members_address (\par
                address_id,\par
                house_number_or_name,\par
                street_name,\par
                city_or_town,\par
                post_code,\par
                country\par
            )\par
            VALUES (\par
                v_next_id,\par
                p_house_number_or_name,\par
                p_street_name,\par
                p_city_or_town,\par
                p_post_code,\par
                p_country\par
            );\par
\par
            -- Return full inserted row\par
            SET o_address_id   = v_next_id;\par
            SET o_house_number_or_name = p_house_number_or_name;\par
            SET o_street_name  = p_street_name;\par
            SET o_city_or_town = p_city_or_town;\par
            SET o_post_code    = p_post_code;\par
            SET o_country      = p_country;\par
\par
            COMMIT;\par
            \par
            SET o_sqlstate = '00000'; -- success\par
\par
            SET v_done = 1; -- Success, exit loop\par
        END;\par
    END WHILE;\par
\par
    -- If all retries failed\par
    IF v_done = 0 THEN\par
        SIGNAL SQLSTATE '45000'\par
            SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'sp_create_address failed after 3 retry attempts.';\par
    END IF;\par
\par
END$$\par
\par
DELIMITER ;\par
\b\f8\lang2057 What it does\par
\par
1. Declares internal variables\par
\par
\b0 v_next_id \f3\u8594?\f9  \f8 the newly generated ID (e.g., "A53")\par
v_max_num \f3\u8594?\f9  \f8 highest numeric portion of existing IDs\par
v_attempts \f3\u8594?\f9  \f8 retry counter\par
v_done \f3\u8594?\f9  \f8 signals success or failure\par
\par
These control ID generation, retry logic, and exit conditions.\par
\par
\par
2. Sets a lock\f9\_wait timeout\par
SET innodb_lock_wait_timeout = 5;\par
Prevents long blocking when another transaction holds a lock on the ID row.\par
\par
\par
3. Implements retry logic\par
A WHILE loop attempts the operation up to 3 times.\par
If a deadlock occurs (SQLSTATE 40001), the procedure rolls back and retries automatically.\par
If any non\_deadlock error occurs, retries stop immediately.\par
If all retries fail, the procedure raises SQLSTATE 45000 to signal retry exhaustion.\par
\par
\par
4. Defines three distinct error handlers\par
\par
Duplicate key handler (SQLSTATE 23000):\par
Triggered when the UNIQUE constraint rejects a duplicate address.\par
Rolls back, returns NULL for addressId, returns the attempted address fields,\par
sets o_sqlstate = '23000', and stops execution.\par
Allows Java to detect \ldblquote ADDRESS EXISTS\rdblquote .\par
\par
Deadlock handler (SQLSTATE 40001):\par
Rolls back and allows the retry loop to continue.\par
No SQLSTATE is returned because the SP retries internally.\par
\par
Generic SQL exception handler (SQLEXCEPTION):\par
Rolls back, clears ALL OUT parameters, sets o_sqlstate = 'HY000',\par
and stops execution.\par
Used for all other SQL errors.\par
\par
\par
5. Starts a transaction\par
START TRANSACTION;\par
Ensures atomicity: either the entire insert succeeds or everything rolls back cleanly.\par
\par
\par
6. Reads the highest existing ID using row\_level locking\par
\f8 SELECT \f9\'85 \f8 FOR UPDATE:\f9\par
Locks the row containing the highest address_id.\par
Prevents concurrent sessions from generating the same next ID.\par
Extracts the numeric portion using SUBSTRING(address_id, 2).\par
\par
\par
7. Generates the next ID\par
If no rows exist:\par
v_next_id = 'A0'\par
Else:\par
v_next_id = CONCAT('A', v_max_num + 1)\par
Produces IDs like:\par
\f8 A0, A1, A2 \f9\'85 \f8 A53, A54\f9\par
\par
\par
8. Inserts the new address\par
INSERT INTO members_address_testing (...)\par
VALUES (v_next_id, p_house_number_or_name, p_street_name, p_city_or_town, p_post_code, p_country);\par
If the address is a duplicate, the UNIQUE constraint triggers SQLSTATE 23000\par
and the duplicate handler runs.\par
\par
\par
9. Returns the full inserted address\par
OUT parameters return:\par
addressId, houseNumberOrName, streetName, cityOrTown, postCode, country\par
o_sqlstate = '00000' on success\par
o_sqlstate = '23000' on duplicate\par
o_sqlstate = 'HY000' on other SQL errors\par
\par
If duplicate:\par
addressId = NULL\par
Other fields = attempted values\par
\par
If other SQL error:\par
All OUT params = NULL\par
\par
\par
10. Commits the transaction\par
COMMIT;\par
Sets o_sqlstate = '00000' to indicate success.\par
Finalizes the insert only if:\par
No deadlocks remain\par
No SQL errors occurred\par
ID generation and insertion succeeded\par
\par
\par
Summary:\par
This stored procedure is now:\par
Transactional  \par
Thread\_safe  \par
Race\_safe  \par
Retry\_protected  \par
Deadlock\_safe  \par
Duplicate\_aware  \par
Timeout\_safe  \par
Fully atomic  \par
Returns full data  \par
Returns duplicate attempts cleanly  \par
Returns SQLSTATE explicitly  \par
Uses prefix A(n+1)  \par
Suitable for real production environments\f0\par
