\b Address Uniqueness \f1\emdash  Notes\par
\b0 What makes an address unique:\par
The system prevents duplicate addresses using a composite unique constraint at the database level. \par
This constraint ensures that the combination of:\par
\par
- house_number_or_name\par
- post_code\par
must be unique across the entire table.\par
\par
\b Why this matters:\b0\par
- Many different addresses share the same postcode.\par
-  Only the full combination of fields reliably identifies a unique physical address.\par
\par
\b How duplicates are prevented:\b0\par
The table defines a composite UNIQUE constraint:\par
    UNIQUE (house_number_or_name, post_code)\par
\par
\b This means:\b0\par
- The database will reject any attempt to insert an address that matches all \f0\lang2057 two\f1\lang9  fields.\par
- The stored procedure does not need to check for duplicates manually.\par
- The controller catches the database integrity exception and returns a clean 409 Conflict response.\par
\par
\b Benefits:\b0\par
- Duplicate prevention is handled by the database engine (fast, safe, race\f9\_\f0 condition\f9\_\f0 proof).\par
- The stored procedure remains focused on ID generation and transactional safety.\par
- The controller provides clear feedback to the frontend when a duplicate address is submitted.\par
